---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Photo from '../components/Photo.astro';
import kevinPhoto from '../assets/photos/kevin-at-the-shed.webp';
---
<Base
  title="Our Story | Windrose Farm"
  description="Where Windrose Farm came from: an aunt who kept bees from the age of sixteen, a long search, and 37 acres of Arizona high desert."
>
  <div class="wrap page-head">
    <h1>Our story</h1>
  </div>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <p>
        Windrose Farm did not begin with the purchase of land. It began
        decades earlier.
      </p>
      <p>
        One of the earliest inspirations was <strong>Aunt Rosie</strong>. She
        took up beekeeping at sixteen and kept at it for the rest of her life.
        On her farm in the Midwest she had a huge garden and a lot of fruit
        trees, raised chickens, tapped her maples for syrup, and pressed her
        apples for cider. She was proud that she farmed organically, and much
        of what she grew went to the local soup kitchen. She was doing all of
        this long before sustainable agriculture became fashionable.
      </p>
      <p>
        Years later, after years of dreaming, planning, and searching, we
        found 37 acres of windswept Arizona high desert. Many people would
        have seen an empty field. We saw possibility.
      </p>
      <Photo
        class="inline"
        src={kevinPhoto}
        alt="Kevin in a sun hat and sunglasses, smiling and giving a thumbs-up in front of the red machine shed"
      >
        Kevin, at the machine shed.
      </Photo>
      <p>
        Our goal is not to create the perfect farm. Our goal is to become
        better stewards every year. Some experiments will succeed. Others
        won't. We'll share both.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2>Who we are</h2>
      <p>
        Windrose is a family project. I'm Kevin, and I write most of what's on
        this site. Nobody lives on the land yet, so the work gets done on
        weekends, fitted around everything else. That's also the main thing
        that's gone wrong so far: time. We don't have enough of it.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you've got questions, or you're working similar ground, write to
        <a href="mailto:hello@windrose.farm">hello@windrose.farm</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2>Why "Windrose"</h2>
      <p>
        The name comes from two places. One is the compass rose on a
        navigator's chart, which is about finding your bearings and reading
        the forces around you. The other is Aunt Rosie.
      </p>
      <p>
        It also fits the property, where the prevailing wind shapes nearly
        every design decision. The idea is to work with the land's seasons
        and limits instead of fighting them.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2>What we're working toward</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Our mission:</strong> to cultivate healthy land, healthy food,
        and healthy community through careful stewardship, regenerative
        agriculture, and a lifelong commitment to learning.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is a family legacy project, not a commercial farm. Over time we
        hope it becomes an apiary, a garden, an orchard and a small vineyard,
        with habitat for wildlife and room for family and friends to gather,
        and that it gets passed on to the next generation.
      </p>
      <p>The values we try to work by:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Stewardship.</strong> We are caretakers, not owners. The job is to leave the land healthier than we found it.</li>
        <li><strong>Sustainability.</strong> Solar power, water conservation, compost, healthy soil.</li>
        <li><strong>Organic practices.</strong> No formal certification, but organic principles wherever practical.</li>
        <li><strong>Patience.</strong> Nature works in seasons and generations. So do we.</li>
        <li><strong>Curiosity.</strong> The farm is a place to keep learning, experimenting and observing.</li>
        <li><strong>Family.</strong> Everything begins and ends with family.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="dedication">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>For Aunt Rosie</h2>
      <p>
        Windrose Farm is lovingly dedicated to Aunt Rosie. May every blossom
        visited by our bees, every tree planted, every garden tended, and
        every harvest gathered honor the quiet example she set.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="next-step">
    <div class="wrap">
      <a class="btn" href="/land/">More about the land →</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
